  "nameStory": "",
  "description": "The Many-rooted Earthball is a small, round fungus with a distinctive warty, brown exterior that resembles a miniature potato. This species belongs to the Sclerodermataceae family, commonly known as earthballs, and is found in New England woodlands.",
  "idTips": [
    {
      "label": "Multiple Root-like Strands",
      "description": "The base of the fruiting body has numerous thin, root-like attachments (rhizomorphs) that anchor it to the soil, which is the namesake feature of this species.",
      "isPrimary": true
    },
    {
      "label": "Warty Brown Cap",
      "description": "The exterior surface is covered with small, distinct warts or bumps and ranges from tan to dark brown in color, creating a textured appearance.",
      "isPrimary": false
    },
    {
      "label": "Small Size",
      "description": "Fruiting bodies typically measure 1-3 cm in diameter, making them noticeably smaller than many other earthball species.",
      "isPrimary": false
    }
  ],
  "habitat": "Many-rooted Earthballs grow in sandy or well-drained soils in deciduous and mixed forests throughout New England, often appearing near tree roots.",
  "seasonality": {
    "spring": "",
    "summer": "",
    "fall": "",
    "winter": ""
  },
  "funFact": "Like other earthballs, Scleroderma polyrhizum is inedible and potentially toxic, with a solid interior that releases a dark powder only when mature.",
  "confusionNotes": null,
